1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,376
Preload describes end-diastolic filling and myocardial stretch; the
school uses ventricular filling volume as a useful simplified proxy.
Afterload describes the load the ventricle must overcome to eject.
Stroke volume is the amount ejected with one beat.

2
00:00:17,376 --> 00:00:35,928
Cardiac output is heart rate multiplied by stroke volume. A monitor
supplies electrical rate, not stroke volume. Therefore a normal rate
cannot establish normal output, and a fast rate cannot be interpreted
without asking what happened to filling and the amount ejected per
beat.

3
00:00:35,928 --> 00:00:56,328
Blood loss can reduce preload and stroke volume, so tachycardia may
initially compensate. Poor contractility after myocardial injury can
reduce output despite available filling. High afterload can make
ejection harder. These mechanisms may coexist rather than fitting a
single tidy category.

4
00:00:56,328 --> 00:01:15,384
Increasing filling helps only within the heart’s physiological
capacity. Excess volume in a failing pump may worsen congestion. Use
the mechanism to organize assessment, but do not convert a conceptual
preload lesson into an automatic fluid instruction for every
hypotensive patient.

5
00:01:15,384 --> 00:01:33,840
Sympathetic activation generally raises rate, contractility and AV
transmission during pain, stress or shock. Vagal activity can slow
sinus activity and AV conduction. The tracing records part of that
response; the history helps explain why it occurred.

6
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:53,040
Review beta-blockers, calcium-channel blockers, digoxin and other
relevant exposures. Vomiting, vagal stimulation or nodal ischemia can
also contribute to slowing. A medication history may explain why a
very ill patient does not develop the expected compensatory
tachycardia.

7
00:01:53,040 --> 00:02:12,936
Hypoxia can increase electrical instability and worsen myocardial
oxygen imbalance. Acid–base disturbance can impair cardiac function
and interact with potassium abnormalities. Neither has one mandatory
ECG appearance, and a single waveform cannot establish blood gas
results.

8
00:02:12,936 --> 00:02:34,248
Consider the whole illness: respiratory failure, sepsis, diabetic
metabolic disturbance or other causes may affect rhythm. Continue
appropriate airway, breathing and circulatory assessment, obtain
available measurements and communicate the suspected mechanism without
claiming laboratory confirmation that you do not have.

9
00:02:34,248 --> 00:02:55,728
The school associates hyperkalemia with peaked T waves, less visible
atrial activity and widening QRS, potentially progressing to severe
conduction disturbance. These changes are variable and need not appear
in a fixed sequence. A dialysis or renal history can sharply increase
concern even without textbook waves.

10
00:02:55,728 --> 00:03:17,256
Hypokalemia can produce flat T waves, prominent U waves and ectopy,
and may increase repolarization risk. An apparent long interval may
partly be a fused T–U complex rather than a clean QT measurement.
Communicate the suspicion and measurement limitation rather than
diagnosing an exact electrolyte value.

11
00:03:17,256 --> 00:03:35,688
Hypothermia can slow the heart and conduction, prolong intervals and
produce an Osborn or J wave. These are useful clues, not universal
requirements or a standalone temperature measurement. Consider
exposure, measured temperature and overall physiology together.

12
00:03:35,688 --> 00:03:52,896
Manage the cold patient through the applicable environmental and
resuscitation pathway while monitoring changes. Handle the patient
appropriately and document the temperature context. Do not force a
temperature-related tracing into a routine normothermic rhythm
explanation.

13
00:03:52,896 --> 00:04:12,864
For each case, propose a mechanism and a finding that would support or
weaken it. A volume-loss story, a pump-failure story and a
vasodilation story may all produce tachycardia, but they do not
justify the same response. ECG is part of that reasoning, not the
entire explanation.

14
00:04:12,864 --> 00:04:31,272
After an intervention through the authorized pathway, reassess
symptoms, pulse, pressure, skin, mentation and the tracing.
Improvement in rate without improvement in perfusion is an incomplete
response. Record the cause you suspected and the evidence that changed
your plan.
